\section{Sharing}{

A collection is shared, like \code{\link[shiny:reactiveValues]{shiny::reactiveValues()}}: after \code{y <- x}, both
names refer to the same collection, and a change made through \verb{$<-},
\verb{[[<-}, \verb{[<-}, \verb{names<-} or \code{reorder()} is seen by everything holding it.
That is what lets one part of an app change a collection while another
reacts to it. Subsetting with \code{[} returns a new collection with its own set
of slots, holding the same reactives, and \code{x[]} copies the whole collection.
So \code{x <- x[order]} gives a reordered copy, which suffices when nothing else
holds \code{x}, while \code{reorder(x, order)} reorders the shared collection itself.
Renaming with \verb{names<-} keeps every slot at its position, as for a list.
}

\section{Lifetime}{

A collection belongs to the session or module it was created in and is
destroyed along with it, as a \code{\link[shiny:reactiveVal]{shiny::reactiveVal()}} is. Reading or changing
it from another module doesn't tie it to that module, so destroying the
module leaves the collection intact. A reactive bound to a slot, however,
still belongs to the module it was created in.
}

\section{Dependencies}{

Reading a slot makes the caller depend on that slot alone. The caller
re-runs when the slot is bound, replaced or removed, but not when other
slots change. This also holds for a slot that does not exist yet, so a
reader re-runs once the slot is added. Reading \code{names()} or \code{length()}
depends on which slots exist and in what order, and \code{as.list()} depends on
that and on every slot. Reordering re-runs readers of \code{names()}, \code{length()},
\code{as.list()} and of slots by position, but not readers of slots by name.

Printing, \code{format()} and \code{str()} make the caller depend on nothing, and they
never call a slot, so they run no computed slot. Outside a reactive
consumer, as at the console, \code{names()} and \code{length()} work as they would
inside \code{\link[shiny:isolate]{shiny::isolate()}} rather than fail.
}

\section{Reactlog}{

In \href{https://rstudio.github.io/reactlog/}{reactlog}, a collection is named
after the variable it was assigned to when created, as a
\code{\link[shiny:reactiveValues]{shiny::reactiveValues()}} object is. After \code{x <- reactives(a = ...)}, the
dependency on slot \code{a} shows as \code{x$a}, the one on which slots exist and in
what order as \code{names(x)}, and those on unnamed slots as \code{x$...1}, \code{x$...2}
and so on. Unnamed slots are numbered in the order they were added rather
than by position, so a label survives reordering. The name is read from
source references, and a collection created without them, as in an
installed package, is named after its class and a number instead, as in
\code{reactives1}.
}

